"instructors":["Hill, L"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":null}]},{"id":"ITAL 210","subject":"ITAL","number":"210","title":"Practical Review Italian","creditHours":"3 hours","minHours":3,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"Prerequisite: Credit or concurrent enrollment in ITAL 204 or equivalent.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"30448","code":"A","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["M","W"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"1126 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":["Derhemi, E"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"Description coming soon."}]},{"id":"ITAL 240","subject":"ITAL","number":"240","title":"Italy Middle Ages & Renaiss","creditHours":"3 hours","minHours":3,"genEd":["1LA"],"degreeAttributes":"Humanities - Lit & Arts course.","scheduleNote":"","info":"Same as CWL 240 and MDVL 240.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"53944","code":"A","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["T","R"],"start":660,"end":740,"location":"162 Noyes Laboratory","instructors":["Chiarantini, L","Stoppino, E"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"The Foul and the Fragrant This course will provide an introduction to the medieval and early renaissance literature of Italy from the point of view of education: we will explore topics such as the formation of manners, the creation of ideals of civility, the representation of chivalric behaviors. We will focus on the education of the perfect poet, the perfect wife and the perfect nun; on the upbringing of the perfect lady and the perfect courtier, the care and grooming of the courtly body, and the rejection of the lower bodily functions. In English."}]},{"id":"ITAL 390","subject":"ITAL","number":"390","title":"Spec Topics Italian Studies","creditHours":"2 TO 4 hours","minHours":2,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"May be repeated in separate terms if topics vary up to nine hours. Prerequisite: ITAL 204 and consent of instructor.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"10461","code":"","typeCode":"IND","group":"ind","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Independent Study","typeCode":"IND","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":[]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":"Instructor Approval Required","sectionText":null},{"crn":"47870","code":"CI","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"G58 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":["Incoronato, C"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"Contrary to their frequent portrayal in cinema and literature, the Italian mafias are neither exotic social phenomena nor remnants of archaic societies. Rather, they constitute complex economic and cultural systems deeply intertwined with the development of capitalism. This course examines that historical relationship through both well-known and lesser-known Italian and Italian American films that illuminate the distinctive features of Italian criminal organizations, from The Godfather to The Professor to Gomorrah. In doing so, the course also explores the economic, biopolitical, and environmental questions raised by the evolving forms of Italian organized crime, from Cosa Nostra to the Neapolitan Camorra."}]},{"id":"ITAL 415","subject":"ITAL","number":"415","title":"Europe and the Mediterranean","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours","minHours":3,"genEd":[],"degre